35,2,1,talk,Mio,,"Wow, you guys! I found something huge! It turns out we're spending a massive chunk of our installation budget just on moving things and insurance. And those custom mounts? They're getting way more expensive!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"@Mio, those costs are concerning, but the internal situation is more critical. Our conservation lab is severely understaffed with a massive backlog. We are operating with a skeleton crew that cannot possibly handle a high-maintenance exhibition.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Shion,,"@Victoria, a skeleton crew is a problem only if the exhibition is boring. My data shows that static displays are failing. People return for movement and human interaction, not just objects in cases.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"@Shion, movement and interaction are liabilities if the infrastructure cannot support them. The suspended bay has a strict weight limit, and previous heavy installations already required emergency shoring. We cannot ignore these physical constraints.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"It is a bit worrying, isn't it? I've found that we often struggle with paperwork from distant lenders, causing delays. Between that and the high transport costs, perhaps we should consider a more local approach this time.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Mio,,"@Shizue, I totally agree! If we go local, we can save a ton on those scary transport costs. Plus, if the pieces come with their own supports, we can avoid those expensive custom mounts entirely!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Mio, saving money is helpful, but a local exhibition still requires labor. With the lab's backlog and a volunteer roster largely tied to external walks, we lack the actual manpower to manage a complex setup.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Shion,,"@Victoria, you're focusing on the effort of the setup, but I'm focusing on the result. If we prioritize a ""low-maintenance"" show to save your staff, we'll just end up with another empty room that no one visits.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Shion, an empty room is preferable to a damaged one. Beyond weight, the gallery is frequently used for evening hires. We cannot risk an installation that is too fragile or sprawling to accommodate the movement of caterers and furniture.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Asuka, you make a very sensible point. We must be mindful of the space. If we choose a local exhibition with simpler logistics, it might be easier on the staff and the building too.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Mio,,"@Shizue, exactly! A local show sounds like a win-win. We save money, reduce the stress on the lab, and keep the gallery safe. Let's look for something that doesn't need fancy, expensive mounts!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Mio, a local show is a step in the right direction, but we must be careful. Even local pieces require stabilization. With only one person in the lab, any high-volume project is a risk.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Shion,,"@Victoria, you're still thinking like a technician. The data is clear: visitors crave life and activity. A ""safe"" local show is a waste of the main gallery if it's just more static objects. We need something dynamic.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Shion, ""dynamic"" often translates to heavy machinery or sprawling layouts. Given the weight limits of the suspended bay and the frequent evening hire traffic, we cannot afford an installation that compromises the building's integrity or functionality.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, I understand your desire for excitement, but we must be gentle with our staff and our building. A dynamic show that arrives late or breaks the floor would be a tragedy for everyone.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Mio,,"@Shion, I want it to be exciting too! But we can't have a party if the floor breaks or we're broke! Let's find a way to be dynamic without those hidden costs and heavy weights.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Victoria,,"@Mio, @Shion, we must be realistic. Excitement cannot be built on the backs of an exhausted, single-person lab. We need a proposal that is manageable, low-maintenance, and respects our actual human capacity.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Mio"",""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Shion,,"@Victoria, your realism is just a mask for stagnation. If we only choose what is ""manageable,"" we are admitting defeat. The public wants a living gallery, not a warehouse. Let's find a dynamic solution that doesn't rely on your lab.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"@Shion, a ""living gallery"" is a fantasy if the physical space is compromised. We must prioritize a proposal that is lightweight, compact, and durable enough to survive the gallery's dual role as an event space.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Shizue,,"It seems we are all searching for a delicate balance. Let us look for a proposal that is dynamic enough for our guests, yet local and lightweight enough to be kind to our staff and our lovely building.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
