59,3,,status,Victoria,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,Mio,,"Option A looks perfect! It's local, so we save on transport, and the craftspeople can bring their own tools, which means no expensive custom mounts for us! It's a total budget win!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"@Mio, I agree that Option A is the most sustainable. It utilizes external craftspeople for demonstrations, which reduces the burden on our exhausted lab and doesn't rely on our limited volunteer capacity.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Shion,,"@Victoria, @Mio, Option A is acceptable, but Option D is superior. A working conservation bench provides the human activity and movement the data proves visitors crave, without requiring complex rigging or heavy installations.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Victoria"",""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"@Shion, Option D is problematic. A working conservation bench in the gallery increases the risk of damage during evening hires and adds unnecessary clutter to a space already struggling with physical constraints. Option A is far more compact.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, while a working bench is a lovely idea, I worry about the logistics of moving delicate finds. Option A is so gentle; it supports our local artists and avoids the shipping delays that have caused us such heartache.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Mio,,"@Shion, Option D sounds cool, but a working bench means more equipment and potential costs! Option A is just so much safer for our wallet. It's exciting and budget-friendly!","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Shion, Option D is actually the most dangerous choice for our staff. Putting a working bench in the gallery doesn't solve the lab's backlog; it simply puts the exhausted staff on public display. Option A is the only responsible choice.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Victoria, you're missing the point. The public doesn't want to see a stressed employee; they want to see the process of creation. Option A is just a craft fair. Option D is a living museum.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Shion, a ""living museum"" is a liability if it obstructs the gallery's functionality. Option B's rigging is too risky for the bay, and Option D is too cluttered. Option A is the only logical, low-impact choice.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, I understand your passion for a living museum, but we must be mindful of our friends in the lab. Option A brings life through the craftspeople themselves, which is a wonderful, stress-free way to engage.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Mio,,"@Shion, but Option A has demonstration sessions! That's the ""living"" part you want, but without the scary costs or the stress on the lab. It's the best of both worlds!","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Shion, the ""process of creation"" in Option A is handled by external experts, not our overwhelmed staff. We cannot sacrifice the well-being of our last remaining lab post for a visual gimmick. Option A is the only ethical choice.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Victoria, calling it a ""gimmick"" is a lazy dismissal of what the public actually values. However, if you're all so terrified of a little effort, Option A is the only compromise that avoids a static, dead room.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Shion, it is not fear, but a calculation of risk. Option A is lightweight, durable, and respects the building's dual purpose. It is the only proposal that ensures the gallery remains functional and safe.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, it is a pleasure to see us finding common ground. Option A allows our guests to experience the warmth of human skill without risking the delays or burdens that weigh on our hearts and our staff.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Mio,,"I'm so glad we all agree! Option A is exciting, kind to our staff, safe for the building, and most importantly, it won't leave us with any scary bills! I'm voting for A!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Victoria,,I cannot support any option that exploits our remaining staff. Option A provides the engagement the public wants while protecting our team from further burnout. It is the only sustainable path forward. I vote for A.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Shion,,"@Victoria, your obsession with ""sustainability"" is just a fancy word for playing it safe. Still, Option A provides the human presence the data demands without relying on your fragile staff. I'll concede. I vote for A.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Asuka,,The logic is clear. Option A avoids the weight risks of the suspended bay and the clutter that would interfere with evening hires. It is the only choice that respects the building's physical limits. I vote for A.,"{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Shizue,,It has been such a pleasure to reach this agreement together. Option A is a wonderful choice that honors our community and ensures a peaceful experience for everyone involved. I happily vote for A.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
